  "narrative": "Initial hypotheses prioritize established dependency logic in lung adenocarcinoma-like cells and binary molecular predictors with adequate cell counts. Negative direction means the exposed group is expected to show stronger dependency (more negative CRISPR score); positive direction means weaker dependency (less negative score). Expected effects include oncogene addiction for KRAS, BRAF V600E, EGFR amplification, and ERBB2 amplification; pathway crosstalk effects such as reduced BRAF dependency in KRAS mutants and increased STAT3 dependency with EGFR amplification; KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 dependency; TP53-loss/MDM2 synthetic relationship; MSI-high/WRN synthetic lethality; SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 synthetic lethality; and MYC-amplification effects on transcriptional and USP7 dependencies. Analyses are restricted to CRISPR-QC cells and DNA-omics-measured samples to avoid imputed molecular states. All are marked unresolved pending discovery evidence and uncertainty.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 1 registered 12 direct comparisons using common binary molecular predictors to satisfy 20-observed cells. Direction convention: -1 = more negative dependency in exposed; +1 = less negative dependency in exposed. Initial focus is mechanistic molecular markers. Later iterations can explore research markers/signatures, culture/technical variables, continuous signatures via interactions, and candidate subgroups based on observed signals."
}

