  "narrative": "Registered new comparisons that refine the three dataset-specific synthetic-lethal/cancer-dependency signals, test their robustness across lineage, library, and culture context, and add mechanistically related broad-exploration tests. For KRAS dependency, the original all-sample association was small and unresolved; a lung adenocarcinoma-only test is a scientifically meaningful refinement. For MSI-high/WRN, SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2, and KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2, I registered direct subgroup mean differences within adenocarcinoma to obtain interpretable within-subgroup effect sizes, and interaction tests by lineage subtype, Ky library, and adherent culture to assess whether these large effects are stable or context-specific. The new ARID1A-loss versus SMARCA2 dependency comparison probes another SWI/SNF complex context, and the KEAP1-loss versus USP7 dependency comparison tests pathway crosstalk around NFE2L2 regulation. All comparisons use CRISPR-QC and DNA-omics-measured cells and are marked unresolved before analysis.",
  "research_notes": "Accepted dataset-specific associations from discovery: H11 MSI-high stronger WRN dependency; H12 SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H13 KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency. Rejected or closed direct claims: H2, H3, H5, H7, H9. H4 direct EGFR amplification/EGFR dependency remains unresolved but closed due inconsistent discovery/validation; H20 may explore subgroup heterogeneity. Unresolved active direct molecular associations: H1 KRAS mutation/KRAS dependency, H6 ERBB2 amplification/ERBB2 dependency, H8 TP53 loss/MDM2 dependency, H10 MYC amplification/USP7 dependency. Existing registered follow-up claims H14-H23 test lineage, research-marker, and EGFR/KRAS subgroup interactions. New registered claims refine accepted effects in adenocarcinoma and test library_ky and adherent-culture heterogeneity; add ARID1A-loss/SMARCA2 and KEAP1-loss/USP7. Plan: run robustness and focused mechanistic tests; if robust large effects remain, consider independent validation; interpret all as dataset-specific associations, not causality."
}

