  "narrative": "The newly delivered independent validation for H11 strongly supports the MSI-high association with stronger WRN dependency. Discovery R11, signed to the negative-dependency hypothesis, was +0.790 with a 95% interval [0.760, 0.820]. Independent validation R26 was +0.797 with an independent interval [0.744, 0.850] under stricter validation-adjusted coverage. Both lower bounds are far above any approximate 10% clinically relevant threshold on the WRN dependency score scale, and the two estimates are highly consistent. I therefore accept H11 as a robust dataset-specific association and close its direct investigation because the central direct claim has now been internally validated. This does not establish causality in cells or generalizable clinical utility; it establishes that the supplied data and supplied independent validation both show a large MSI-high versus WRN dependency-score difference.\n\nWith H11 replicated, the strongest evidence in the project remains the trio of large dependency-score associations: MSI-high cells show stronger WRN dependency (H11, replicated; H25 adenocarcinoma-restricted), SMARCA4-loss cells show stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H12; H26 adenocarcinoma-restricted), and KEAP1-loss cells show weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H13; H27 adenocarcinoma-restricted). The first of these is now the most secure claim in the accepted set. The current context results are mostly reassuring but incomplete. Lineage-subtype interaction tests suggest no strong evidence of SMARCA4-loss effect modification by subtype (H15 rejected), while WRN and NFE2L2 subtype interactions remain imprecise but low priority. Library ky context tests showed no meaningful WRN interaction (H28 rejected) and an imprecise near-null NFE2L2 interaction (H30 deferred); the SMARCA2 ky interaction is borderline and unresolved (H29), so the newly registered direct ky subgroup test H36 is important. Adherent-culture interactions are pending, and I registered direct adherent-subgroup mean differences for WRN/MSI-high, SMARCA2/SMARCA4-loss, and NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss because these will provide interpretable within-culture effect sizes rather than only interaction contrasts.\n\nNegative findings remain important: KRAS mutation on BRAF dependency, BRAF V600E on BRAF dependency, EGFR amplification on STAT3 dependency, the original KEAP1-loss stronger-NFE2L2 hypothesis, MYC amplification on POLR2A dependency, the SMARCA4-loss by subtype interaction, and the MSI-high by ky library interaction for WRN were rejected as not supporting clinically meaningful effects in their registered directions. Unresolved uncertainty remains for direct KRAS/EGFR/ERBB2/TP53/MYC associations, the EGFR/KRAS-wild-type subgroup interaction, low-priority library and subtype interactions, ARID1A-loss on SMARCA2, KEAP1-loss on USP7, and the pending context analyses. The most informative next analyses are the direct ky tests H36-H38, the new direct adherent tests, and then the marker/context tests. If the context tests remain broadly consistent, the next independent validation should prioritize H12, because it is a large mechanistically meaningful association without independent replication; H13 would be the next candidate. If a context test shows strong attenuation or heterogeneity, that should be investigated before spending additional validation on that pathway."
}

