  "narrative": "Registered new mechanistically motivated comparisons targeting outcome–predictor pairs not yet explored, while retaining the accepted WRN/MSI-high, SMARCA2/SMARCA4-loss, and NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss findings for follow-up context checks. The new RIT1 panel tests upstream RTK-to-RAS/MAPK signaling logic: KRAS and BRAF mutant cells are hypothesized to be less dependent on upstream RIT1, so their dependency scores should be less negative than in wild-type cells (positive raw difference), whereas ERBB2 or EGFR amplification could reflect receptor-driven signaling that still uses RIT1 and thus could be associated with stronger RIT1 dependency (more negative scores). The EGFR/RIT1 direction is deliberately uncertain because the direct EGFR/dependency_EGFR claim was not externally consistent, but testing RIT1 asks a distinct mechanistic question. MYC amplification versus dependency_DHX9 tests whether transcription-factor amplification creates a helicase/transcription-stress dependency beyond the null MYC/POLR2A result. TP53 loss versus dependency_RPL11 and dependency_USP7 extends p53-pathway dependency logic: TP53 loss should reduce reliance on p53-pathway nodes, giving less negative scores. The MDM2 interaction refinement asks whether the weak TP53-loss effect is modified by MTAP loss, a co-deleted p53-region alteration that could alter MDM2-pathway context. Finally, EGFR amplification versus TMED10 probes whether RTK-amplified cells are more dependent on secretory-pathway cargo trafficking. These proposals improve broad coverage of the untested dependency scores and provide falsifiable alternatives to the current accepted synthetic-lethal associations. The next analysis phase should prioritize these new tests together with the highest-value pending context tests: direct ky-library SMARCA2 dependency and direct adherent-culture tests for the accepted associations. Marker D/E/F tests and lower-priority KRAS subtype/culture interactions can be deferred unless they become mechanistically or robustness-priority based on results. All claims remain dataset-specific associations; validation will be used to distinguish replication from incidental dataset structure."
