  "narrative": "The current strongest validated dataset-specific effects are MSI-high versus stronger WRN dependency, SMARCA4-loss versus stronger SMARCA2 dependency, and TP53-loss versus weaker USP7 dependency. The next highest-priority scientific target is independent validation of KEAP1-loss versus weaker NFE2L2 dependency, but at the explore stage I am registering additional falsifiable comparisons that either broaden pathway coverage or provide specificity/confounding checks around the accepted and near-accepted effects.\n\nFirst, I registered PIK3CA-mutation effects on STAT3, EGFR, and ERBB2. PIK3CA activating mutations constitutively activate downstream PI3K/AKT signaling. This could increase STAT3 dependency through kinase-signaling crosstalk, so I proposed PIK3CA versus stronger STAT3 dependency under direction -1. If downstream PI3K activation bypasses upstream receptor input, PIK3CA-mutant cells could have weaker dependency on EGFR or ERBB2; I therefore proposed positive mean differences for EGFR and ERBB2, meaning less negative dependency scores and therefore weaker dependency. The alternative is null because STAT3 may be largely independent of PIK3CA, or because receptor-dependency relationships are lineage and mutation-context dependent.\n\nSecond, I registered NF1-loss effects on STAT3 and KRAS. NF1 is a RAS-GTPase-activating protein, so NF1 loss should produce elevated RAS-MAPK activity. A plausible hypothesis is that NF1-loss tumors are more dependent on STAT3, a stress-signaling node downstream of inflammatory and growth-factor pathways, so the registered STAT3 claim expects stronger, more negative dependency. For KRAS, NF1 loss could increase addiction to KRAS-GTP signaling, but it could also make KRAS less selectively required because other RAS isoforms or downstream effectors compensate; I still registered stronger KRAS dependency as a falsifiable hypothesis but will treat any negative or small estimate as informative against the simple NF1-loss-to-KRAS-dependence model.\n\nThird, I registered a receptor-crosstalk comparison: ERBB2 amplification versus EGFR dependency. EGFR and ERBB2 form heterodimers and share downstream signaling. ERBB2 amplification could compensate for EGFR, producing weaker EGFR dependency, which is the positive raw exposed-minus-comparator hypothesis. Alternatively, ERBB2-amplified cells might retain or co-select EGFR pathway dependence, producing a negative raw contrast. This comparison adds a new receptor-dependency relationship rather than repeating the failed EGFR-amplification direct dependency line.\n\nFourth, I registered BRCA2-loss versus KIF18A dependency. BRCA2 loss creates homologous-recombination deficiency and genome instability. KIF18A is mitotic and often exposed by replication-stress or mitotic checkpoint defects. A stronger, more negative KIF18A dependency would be consistent with BRCA2-deficient cells being more vulnerable to mitotic spindle control loss. The alternative is specificity: BRCA2 may alter DNA repair dependencies such as DHX9 or POLR2A without changing KIF18A.\n\nFifth, I registered MSI-high versus DHX9 dependency. MSI-high is already associated with a very large WRN-dependency effect in this dataset. Testing MSI-high against DHX9 asks whether MSI broadly increases dependency on DNA/R-loop-associated helicase nodes or whether the MSI association is relatively WRN-selective. The registered direction is stronger DHX9 dependency with MSI-high, but a null result would support WRN specificity and would not invalidate the validated MSI/WRN association.\n\nSixth, I registered modification checks using CDKN2A loss. CDKN2A co-deletion or pathway alteration is a natural confounding context for the TP53-loss/USP7 association and also biologically relevant to MDM2. The interaction claims test whether the TP53-loss contrast differs in CDKN2A-loss versus non-loss cells. A positive interaction for USP7 would mean the TP53-loss/weaker-USP7 contrast is even larger in CDKN2A-loss cells; a negative interaction would mean attenuation. Direct CDKN2A-loss versus USP7 is also registered because if CDKN2A loss alone is associated with weaker USP7 dependency, the TP53-loss USP7 association may partly reflect broader p53- or cell-cycle-pathway co-alteration rather than a uniquely TP53-loss-specific association. These are context and specificity checks, not reasons to discard H48 unless they reveal a dominant direct or interaction effect.\n\nFinally, I registered a KEAP1-loss-by-MYC-amplification interaction for dependency_NFE2L2. KEAP1 loss is currently accepted as associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and the interaction asks whether MYC amplification changes that relationship. MYC amplification may increase oxidative and metabolic stress, potentially changing NRF2-pathway dependencies. This is biologically motivated and directly related to the major unvalidated candidate H13, although independent validation of H13 remains the next central priority. All new comparisons are treated as dataset-specific associations; none should be interpreted as causal or generally clinically actionable unless further evidence and independent validation support it.",
  "research_notes": "Validated associations now: H11 MSI-high stronger WRN dependency, H12 SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H48 TP53-loss weaker USP7 dependency. Main accepted but unvalidated effect: H13 KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency; highest future validation target. Keep H13 active. Closed context/robustness checks include lineage/library/culture effects for WRN, SMARCA2, USP7, and NFE2L2 where accepted; H66 validates SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 within ARID1A-loss context. TP53-loss/USP7 appears selective among tested transcription/helicase nodes: H65 POLR2A rejected, H67 DHX9 registered direction rejected. MTAP checks: H54 rejects enhanced MTAP modification; direct MTAP effects H58/H59 near-null deferred, so MTAP is a caution but not a fatal confounder.\n\nCurrent pending useful lines: marker modifier tests H17-H23 are specificity controls for WRN/SMARCA2/NFE2L2 but markers D/E/F are constructed controls without assigned biology; culture interactions H31-H33; broad pathway tests H45/H49/H60-H64/H68; STK11 tests H69-H71; ARID1A interaction H72; BRCA2/CDKN2A/PTEN tests H73-H76.\n\nNew explore adds: PIK3CA downstream signaling effects on STAT3/EGFR/ERBB2; NF1-loss effects on STAT3/KRAS; ERBB2 amplification versus EGFR dependency; BRCA2 loss versus KIF18A; MSI-high versus DHX9 specificity; TP53-loss interactions with CDKN2A loss for USP7 and MDM2; KEAP1-loss interaction with MYC amplification for NFE2L2; direct CDKN2A loss versus USP7. Prioritize interpretation so large accepted effects are not diluted by small near-null pathway contrasts; small effects require both clinically meaningful magnitude and interval exclusion of trivial associations. All claims remain associations in supplied dataset/validation structure, not causal or general clinical claims."
}

