  "narrative": "There are no assessments_due at this explore stage, so I am not changing existing scientific conclusions. The accepted validated core is MSI-high versus stronger WRN dependency, SMARCA4-loss versus stronger SMARCA2 dependency, TP53-loss versus weaker USP7 dependency, and KEAP1-loss versus weaker NFE2L2 dependency. The next useful comparisons should test whether these large associations are biologically specific, robust to CRISPR-screen quality, and not explained by common co-alterations or pathway bypass contexts.  \n\nI registered co-alteration refinements for the two most important non-MRE pathway dependencies: TP53-loss by SMARCA4-loss for USP7 dependency, and KEAP1-loss by TP53-loss for NFE2L2 dependency. These ask whether the main dependency contrast is enlarged or attenuated in a relevant co-altered tumor-suppressor context. A large attenuation would suggest that correlated losses confound the main association; a large enhancement would define a context where the dependency is especially strong. Because I expect independence more than a strong interaction, the anticipated direction is uncertain, and I will only accept a meaningful interaction if the signed effect is large and the interval remains above the practical dependency benchmark.  \n\nI registered PI3K/RAS/receptor crosstalk checks: PIK3CA mutation effects on EGFR dependency modified by KRAS mutation, and ERBB2 amplification effects on ERBB2 dependency modified by PIK3CA mutation. If downstream PI3K activation bypasses upstream receptor input, receptor-dependency differences should be smaller in PIK3CA-mutant cells, giving a negative interaction. Conversely, if receptor pathway activation remains required, estimates will be near zero or positive. These comparisons broaden beyond the earlier EGFR-amplification line, which failed validation, and test pathway redundancy in a falsifiable way.  \n\nI registered CRISPR-activity robustness interactions using high Cas9 activity, cas9_activity_pct >= 75%, for each core association: MSI-high/WRN, SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2, TP53-loss/USP7, and KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2. Cas9 activity is a measured technical predictor, so a dependency association that depends only on high-Cas9 cells would be more screen-quality driven, whereas an association similar across high and lower Cas9 strata would be more robust. I expect null or small interactions, and these should not replace the central main effects unless they are large and consistently signed.  \n\nFinally, I registered direct STK11-loss versus WRN and SMARCA2 dependencies as specificity controls. STK11 loss was previously null for the KEAP1/NFE2L2 contrast, but direct tests against WRN and SMARCA2 clarify whether STK11 co-alteration is broadly associated with these validated dependencies. I expect null effects; large effects would indicate broader lung cancer context dependence and would require revisiting the specificity of the main effects. All proposals are same-dataset association tests and will be interpreted as dataset-specific associations, not causal mechanisms or guaranteed clinical biomarkers.",
